**Definition 1.2 (The unchanged scalar-port problem).** A legal scalar
port is one fixed composite $T=\operatorname{Dec}\circ\operatorname{Enc}$,
where $F$ is finite and

$$
\operatorname{Enc}:M_5\longrightarrow\bigoplus_{f\in F}M_1,
\qquad \operatorname{Dec}((y_f)_f)=\sum_f\operatorname{Dec}_f(y_f),
\qquad \operatorname{Dec}_f:M_1\longrightarrow M_5
\tag{1.2}
$$

are CPTP, with every decoder TP on its whole scalar block. The design
precedes the input and the future policy. Every quantum path traverses
the port; no original input, entangled assistance or bypass is retained.
Finite classical flags are free for this quantum dimension criterion,
are used in decoding, and are not additionally exposed to the later
policy. Let $\mathfrak C_1$ contain all such complex channels, and set

$$
e(T)=\sup_{\rho\in\mathcal D(\mathbb C^5)}
\sup_{\pi\ {\rm actual,finite}}
\operatorname{TV}(P_\rho^\pi,P_{T(\rho)}^\pi),
\qquad \varepsilon_1=\inf_{T\in\mathfrak C_1}e(T).
\tag{1.3}
$$

Replacement occurs once before the common protocol. TV is half the
$\ell^1$ difference, with its probability-measure version for random
controls. CPTP legality includes all reference extensions; the
performance criterion tests system histories only. No trace-distance
or diamond-norm objective replaces (1.3). No reality, covariance,
unitality, self-adjointness or projector restriction is imposed on the
original competitors.

